sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch05-23> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The undulating membrane of trypanosomes and related flagellates forms when the flagellum lies near and parallel to the body and the fluid surface protoplasm is drawn up as a film along it, as a soap-film would be; the membrane is thus a liquid film of constant mean curvature whose undulations are produced by molecular tensions, not by its own active mobility.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The trypanosome undulating membrane is a liquid film drawn up along a flagellum";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "1/r_1 + 1/r_2 = \\text{const} (undulating membrane as a liquid film)";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter V, “The Forms of Cells”; topic “Undulating membranes of flagellates”. Scope: trypanosomes, Trichomonas and related flagellates, and spermatozoa with undulating membranes"
}
